course: dictionary
lesson: 1088
title: Brachiation: swinging through the trees
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A new word often hides its meaning in the words around it. Look for the clues. «Swinging arm over arm is called brachiation.» The phrase swinging arm over arm tells you how the movement works, so brachiation must be a way of moving by swinging.
explain: Now test your idea. Put a meaning in place of the hard word: «Swinging arm over arm is called moving through the trees by swinging.» It still makes sense, so your meaning fits.
explain: The words in a sentence can also tell you where a word is used. «Gibbons are the fastest apes in the trees.» The phrase in the trees tells you that brachiation happens high up, among the branches.
fact: Brachiation is a way of moving through trees by swinging from branch to branch with the arms.
fact: Gibbons, orangutans and spider monkeys all move by brachiation.
fact: «Gibbons are the fastest apes in the trees, reported at around 50 kilometres per hour.»
fact: Brachiate is the verb: «Gibbons brachiate from tree to tree.» Brachiation is the noun for the movement itself.
fact: Children on monkey bars are doing a kind of brachiation when they swing from bar to bar.
exercise: «Swinging arm over arm is called brachiation.» What does brachiation mean? | Sleeping high up in a tree | *Swinging from branch to branch with the arms | Climbing a trunk using only the legs | Jumping into a pond
exercise: Which animals move by brachiation? | *Gibbons and orangutans | Horses and cows | Fish and frogs | Snakes and worms
exercise: Which word in «Gibbons brachiate from tree to tree» is the verb? | *brachiate | tree | from | Gibbons
test: Which action is a good example of brachiation? | Hopping on one leg | *Swinging arm over arm from one branch to the next | Walking slowly along the ground | Lying still on a branch
test: «Children on the monkey bars are doing a kind of brachiation.» Why? | They are running on the ground | *They swing from bar to bar using their arms | They are sleeping on the bars | They are swimming in a pool
exam: Which sentence uses the verb form correctly? | *Spider monkeys brachiate through the rainforest. | Spider monkeys brachiation through the rainforest. | Spider monkeys brachiate rainforest. | Spider monkeys are brachiation the rainforest.
